-## Subtopics
-
-### View Direction Encoding
-A variant where the encoding is applied to the viewing direction vector $(\theta, \phi)$ instead of or in addition to position. This allows the MLP to model view-dependent effects like specular highlights. Typically uses fewer frequency bands (e.g., 4) than position encoding [S4].
-
-### SIREN Positional Encoding
-In SIREN-based architectures, Fourier encoding is often used as a pre-processing step before the first SIREN layer, or the SIREN layers themselves act as a learnable Fourier feature extractor. The explicit Fourier encoding provides a fixed basis, while SIREN layers learn to combine them [S1, S4].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot D \cdot F)$, where $N$ is the batch size, $D$ is the input dimension, and $F$ is the number of frequency bands. The operation is element-wise and highly parallelizable on GPUs.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot D \cdot F)$ for the output tensor. The frequency bands are pre-computed and stored once.
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot D \cdot F)$, where $N$ is the batch size, $D$ is the input dimension, and $F$ is the number of frequency bands [G]. The operation is element-wise and highly parallelizable on GPUs [G].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot D \cdot F)$ for the output tensor [G]. The frequency bands are pre-computed and stored once [G].
 - **Measured Performance:** In NeRF, using 10 frequency bands for position and 4 for view directions allows the MLP to reconstruct high-frequency details like text and fine geometry that a raw MLP cannot [S2, S4].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable, and effective at overcoming spectral bias in MLPs.
-- **Limitations:** The frequencies are fixed and not learned. If the scene contains frequencies outside the range of the chosen bands, details will be lost. The output dimension grows linearly with the number of bands, which can increase the size of the MLP's input layer [G].
-- **When to Use:** When training an MLP to represent a continuous signal (like a neural radiance field) that contains high-frequency details.
-- **When Not to Use:** When the signal is very smooth and low-frequency, or when memory constraints prevent the increased input dimensionality.
